---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const growthCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'p*(1 + r)^x', label: 'compounded', color: '#064e3b', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: 'p*(1 + r*x)', label: 'simple interest', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.5)' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'principal p', min: 1000, max: 100000, step: 500, value: 10000 },
    { name: 'r', label: 'annual rate r', min: 0, max: 0.15, step: 0.005, value: 0.08, unit: 'as decimal' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 40, label: 'years' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/foundational/compound-interest">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Finance is the craft of moving value through time — and its first law is that
    <strong>money today is worth more than money later</strong>, because money today can earn
    money. Compound interest is what happens when earnings themselves start earning: growth on
    growth, year after year. It is the most important equation in personal finance, and it cuts
    both ways — it compounds debts exactly as enthusiastically as savings.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the same rate, two maths — the gap between the curves is compounding, and it widens forever" widget="plotter" config={growthCfg} />

  <p>
    Slide the rate r: at 8%, compounded money roughly <strong>doubles every 9 years</strong> —
    the curve steepens noticeably by year 30. Simple interest stays a straight line forever;
    compounding bends it into an exponential.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule of 72</h2>
  <p>
    Divide 72 by the annual percentage rate to get the doubling time: 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years, 72 ÷ 12 =
    6 years, 72 ÷ 3 = 24 years (which is why "risk-free" savings at 3% barely outpace inflation's
    own compounding). The same rule runs the other way: a credit card at 36% APR doubles what you
    owe in two years.
  </p>

  <h2>Consequences to internalise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Time beats timing.</strong> Compounding rewards the years you are in the market, not the days you pick. Starting ten years earlier usually beats picking better funds.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtract inflation.</strong> Growth only counts above the inflation curve you met in the economics lesson — "real" return is what compounds after prices.</li>
    <li><strong>Fees compound too.</strong> A 2% annual fee quietly removes about half a lifetime's gains: 6% growth with 2% fees compounds at 4%.</li>
    <li><strong>Debt is compounding against you</strong> — the minimum-payment trap is exponential arithmetic pointed the wrong way.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bogle, The Little Book of Common Sense Investing; standard treatments of present value (e.g. Bodie, Kane & Marcus).</p>
</TopicPage>
